#use(std/code)

#section{Sections}::

#code{#section} takes the title as its first body and the content as the
rest — and detects the heading level automatically: a top-level section is
h1, a section inside another section's content is h2, and so on. Depth comes
from the context (see the contexts page), not from an argument.

The three forms:

#list
	#item: #code|{#section{Title}}| — heading only.
	#item: #code|{#section{Title}::}| — title plus hanging content (the idiomatic page shape).
	#item: #code|{#section{Title}{inline content}}| — title plus inline content.

Siblings vs nesting — a section nested in another's #emph{content} goes one
level deeper; a section after the first one's content ends is a sibling:

#code(lang: "plain")
	#section{Alpha}{Intro to alpha. #section{Alpha One}{A nested section.}}
	#section{Beta}{A sibling section, back to the same level.}

Live (this page's title is already one level, so Alpha renders one deeper):

#section{Alpha}{Intro to alpha. #section{Alpha One}{A nested section.}}
#section{Beta}{A sibling section, back to the same level.}

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: #code|{#section{Title}}| followed by an indented block is #strong{not a form}: block bodies require the call to be alone on its line, and the title brace disqualifies it. Use #code|{#section{Title}::}|.
	#item: Nesting past h6 (depth 7) is an explicit error.
	#item: Each section records itself for #code{#toc} at collect time and gets an #code{id} anchor (#code{sec-1}, #code{sec-2}, …) unique across the whole doc-set.
	#item: With #code{::}, a #emph{later} #code|{#section{…}::}| at the same indentation nests inside the earlier one — within one file you cannot "close" a hanging section except by dedent. Sibling top-level sections in one file need inline bodies or separate files.
